==============================================================================
S3  What the clip surface does in a FIELD formulation div[K(q) grad Psi] = 4 pi G rho
==============================================================================
In spherical symmetry the field equation integrates exactly:
   K Psi' r^2 = G M(<r)   =>   Psi' = G M /(K r^2)
so the density a Newtonian observer would infer is
   rho_eff = rho/K - M K'/(4 pi r^2 K^2),   K' = alpha p q^(p-1) q'.
q' jumps across the clip surface, so rho_eff jumps.  There is NO delta
function: the force and the flux are continuous, the inferred DENSITY is not.
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=1.0   p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=1.0   p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   3.487e+06 Msun/kpc^3 =      37.7 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=1.0   p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=3.0   p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=3.0   p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   1.046e+07 Msun/kpc^3 =       113 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=3.0   p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=10.0  p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=10.0  p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   3.487e+07 Msun/kpc^3 =       377 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=100000 alpha=10.0  p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=1.0   p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=1.0   p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   3.693e+06 Msun/kpc^3 =      3.75 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=1.0   p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=3.0   p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=3.0   p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   1.108e+07 Msun/kpc^3 =      11.2 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=3.0   p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=10.0  p=0.5   [rho_eff] =         inf Msun/kpc^3 =       inf x the local baryon density   (DIVERGENT: q^(p-1) -> inf at q = 0, so rho_eff has an integrable but unbounded spike)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=10.0  p=1.0   [rho_eff] =   3.693e+07 Msun/kpc^3 =      37.5 x the local baryon density   (finite jump)
   rho_ref=1e+06  alpha=10.0  p=2.0   [rho_eff] =           0 Msun/kpc^3 =         0 x the local baryon density   (no jump: q^(p-1) vanishes at the surface)

READ THIS OFF: at p = 1 the clip puts a step in the INFERRED dark
density of order alpha |dln rho/dln r| M/(4 pi r^3) -- comparable to
the baryon density itself.  At p > 1 the step is exactly zero; at
p < 1 it diverges.  So p >= 1 is not a preference, it is a requirement,
and p = 1 still needs the clip rounded.

